      "title": "Plotting collected data: cv2.plot and the CV Chart nodes",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Pictures of pixels vs pictures of numbers.\n\n'Preview CV Array' answers 'what does this array look like as an image'. For COLLECTED DATA - a score per detected thing, a histogram, a metric per method - that is the wrong question: the array is a handful of numbers whose meaning is in their VALUES and in what each index refers to. Rendered as pixels a 5x1 score matrix is five pixels, and 'normalize'/'heatmap' rescale to the array's own min/max, which throws away the absolute scale that made the numbers mean something. color_scale labels the colour ramp but cannot name an index or print a value.\n\nTwo renderers, on purpose:\n'CV Plot 2D (cv2.plot)' is OpenCV's own plotting module. It draws ONE series into an ordinary IMAGE - so it saves, composites and diffs like any other image. No labels, no legend, no tooltips; that is the module's ceiling, not an omission.\n'CV Chart Series / Scatter / Matrix' draw in the node (Apache ECharts): hover for exact values, box-zoom, save a PNG from the toolbox. The Python node builds the WHOLE chart definition and hands it to the frontend, so the same definition comes back out of the 'spec' output - wire it into 'Preview as Text' to read exactly what was charted.\n\nSection C is the label-list pattern from workflow 12: ONE 'Input Text' list of ';'-separated names feeds the scatter's class names, BOTH axes of the confusion matrix, and (via the index Draw Labels prints on each cluster) the picture. Name i, series i, row i and the 'i' drawn on the blob are the same thing - that is the mapping a colour bar cannot give you.\n\nTry: set 'value_range' on the channel-mean chart to 'auto (data range)' and watch a 6-unit difference between channels look like a landslide; switch 'color_by' to 'value (gradient)'; drop the histogram's bins to 8 and read the counts off the bars."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
